  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nQuiet and dry conditions will persist through at least early\nSunday under high pressure. The combination of radiational \ncooling tonight and northerly flow will allow lows to fall into \nthe mid-30s for parts of the region. Frost potential is slightly\nelevated across the ridges and areas northeast of Pittsburgh \n(near I-80); Frost Advisory remains in effect from midnight to \n9am Tuesday. Outside of the advisory area, some isolated pockets\nof frost are possible. Added a mention of patchy frost for\nthese areas. A strengthening southwest flow later this week \nwill returner warm temperatures and effectively end the frost \nthreat into the weekend. \n\nThe next chance for rain will depend on the development and\ntrack of a potential tropical disturbance. The National \nHurricane Center gives the system a 70% chance of formation over\nthe next seven days in the southwest Gulf. Given the noted\nuncertainty, rain chances range from 20-50% Sunday through \nTuesday. Some model solutions have little to no rainfall if the\nsystem fizzles out and/or tracks east before venture north \ntowards the Ohio River Valley. However, there are a few \nensembles tracking the remnants of the disturbance into portions\nof our region and totals could exceed an inch. Temperature\nforecast carry similar uncertainty overt the weekend. Drier \nscenarios support highs in the low to mid-70s, while wetter \nsolutions favor highs in the low to mid-60s.",
